.docname {Logging}
.include {docs}

Karbone is a library: it stays quiet by default and never decides where your logs go.

## Facade and backend

Karbone logs through [kotlin-logging](https://github.com/oshai/kotlin-logging) 8 (`io.github.oshai:kotlin-logging-jvm`), which
comes as a transitive dependency. kotlin-logging does **not** pull `slf4j-api`: it picks a backend from what is already on your
classpath.

| On your classpath                       | Where Karbone's logs go                                                          |
| --------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `org.slf4j:slf4j-api` (Spring Boot, most apps) | SLF4J, hence Logback, Log4j2, JUL... through your usual binding                |
| Logback only                            | Logback directly                                                                 |
| Nothing                                 | kotlin-logging's built-in direct logger (console), or JUL depending on its detection |

In practice: if your application already logs, Karbone follows without any extra dependency. For a bare JVM program, add
`org.slf4j:slf4j-api` plus a binding to control the output.

## Logger names

All loggers live under `com.ekino.oss.karbone`, so one line tunes the whole SDK:

| Logger                                                | What it covers                                                   |
| ----------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `com.ekino.oss.karbone.internal.http.JdkHttpTransport` | Every HTTP call: method, URL, status, retries                    |
| `com.ekino.oss.karbone.internal.DefaultRenders`        | Rendering decisions: hash-first upload, conversion fallback     |

## What is logged, at which level

| Level   | Event                                                                                       | Example                                                                 |
| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `WARN`  | A transport failure that will be retried (only with a `RetryPolicy.OnTransportError`)        | `Transport failure on POST https://api.carbone.io/render/3e66…?download=true, retrying in 200ms (attempt 1/2)` with the exception |
| `DEBUG` | Each request, then its status                                                                | `POST https://api.carbone.io/render/3e66…?download=true` / `… -> HTTP 200` |
| `DEBUG` | Hash-first path: the template is unknown to Carbone and gets uploaded before a retry         | `Template 3e66… unknown to Carbone, uploading invoice.docx then retrying` |
| `DEBUG` | `convert` fell back to `data: {}` for a v4-behaving server                                   | `Server requires a data field for conversion, retrying with an empty data-set` |

Nothing is logged at `INFO` or above in the normal path: a successful render is silent. Failures are not logged either; they come
back to you as a `KarboneError` (or a `KarboneException` from the blocking facade) and you decide whether and how to log them.
`KarboneError.describe()` gives a one-line, human-readable summary suited to a log message.

## What is never logged

- The `Authorization` header or the token.
- Request bodies: your `data`, `complement`, `translations`, template metadata.
- Template content and rendered documents.
- Response bodies, except the excerpt kept inside `KarboneError.Unexpected.body` (up to 2000 characters) that *you* may choose to log.

Note that at `DEBUG` the URLs contain template ids (SHA-256 of your templates) and, in the two-step flow, render ids. A render id is a
single-use credential for `GET /render/{renderId}`, so treat `DEBUG` logs of the transport logger as moderately sensitive.

## Configuration examples

Spring Boot (`application.yml`):

```yaml
logging:
  level:
    com.ekino.oss.karbone: WARN # production default
    com.ekino.oss.karbone.internal.http: DEBUG # trace every Carbone call while investigating
```

Logback (`logback.xml`):

```xml
<logger name="com.ekino.oss.karbone" level="WARN"/>
<logger name="com.ekino.oss.karbone.internal.http.JdkHttpTransport" level="DEBUG"/>
```

Log4j2 (`log4j2.xml`), with `log4j-slf4j2-impl` on the classpath:

```xml
<Logger name="com.ekino.oss.karbone" level="warn"/>
```

## Correlating calls

Karbone does not touch the MDC. Since its API is suspending, MDC values set by the caller are only visible inside Karbone's log lines
if you propagate them across coroutine dispatch, for example with `kotlinx-coroutines-slf4j`:

```kotlin
withContext(MDCContext()) { karbone.renders.render(template, data) }
```

To correlate a render with the Carbone side, keep the `RenderId` returned by `renders.start` in your own logs; there is no request id
header in Carbone's responses today.
